---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Container from "@components/Container.astro";
import PageLayout from "@layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import ArrowCard from "@components/ArrowCard.astro";
import Link from "@components/Link.astro";
import DevHint from "@components/DevHint.astro";
import { SITE, HOME} from "@consts";
import { Icon } from "@astrojs/starlight/components";

const blog = (await getCollection("blog"))
  .filter(post => !post.data.draft)
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf())
  .slice(0,SITE.NUM_POSTS_ON_HOMEPAGE);

---

<PageLayout title={HOME.TITLE} description={HOME.DESCRIPTION}>
  <Container>
    <div class="space-y-16">

      <!-- Purpose: the intent, before the identity. -->
      <section>
        <DevHint section="Purpose (hero)">
          <p>
            Your own sentence is already the closest thing on the site to a statement of
            purpose — it just used to sit third, underneath the greeting. It now leads.
          </p>
          <p>
            The question the stewardship model asks of it: <em>thoughtfulness and
            authenticity toward what?</em> As written, the sentence names a quality of the
            work without naming what makes that quality obligatory. That's the seam where
            worldview would enter — and per your own "subtlety" pillar, it enters as a
            reason, not as vocabulary. Something in the register of <em>why</em> honest
            technology is owed to people, rather than merely preferred.
          </p>
          <p>
            Also decide whether "insanely great" survives. It's a Steve Jobs borrowing, and
            it's the one phrase here pointed at the artifact's impressiveness rather than at
            the person receiving it.
          </p>
        </DevHint>
        <article class="space-y-4">
          <p class="animate">
            I believe in building insanely great technology that inspires thoughtfulness and authenticity.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <!-- Provenance: the unfolding of vocation, not a list of achievements. -->
      <section class="animate space-y-6">
        <DevHint section="Provenance (about)">
          <p>
            The greeting, the turtle, and your work history moved here — identity after
            intent. Your sentences are unchanged.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two things to resolve:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>"Right now I'm building Velovision" is now false</strong> — you said
              you're on <Link href="https://ayni.health" external>ayni.health</Link>. Your
              own truthfulness pillar makes this the highest-priority edit on the site,
              independent of any Christian reframing.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>"Most of my work so far has been..."</strong> is a factual résumé
              line. The Provenance model asks for the unfolding of a vocation instead:
              not the sequence of jobs, but what was being pursued through them, and what
              you came to understand that you hadn't before. Cycling safety → clinical
              safety is a continuity, if you want to name it as one.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </DevHint>
        <div class="flex flex-col items-center space-y-4">
          <div class="animate">
            <img src="/turtle.png" alt="Turtle" class="w-16 h-16 rounded" />
          </div>
          <h4 class="animate font-semibold text-black dark:text-white">
            Hi, I'm Jason <span class="text-xl">👋🏻</span>
          </h4>
        </div>
        <article class="space-y-4">
          <p class="animate">
            Most of my work so far has been at the intersection of cycling and computer vision. Right now I'm building Velovision.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <!-- Contributions: work as service rendered. -->
      <section class="animate space-y-6">
        <DevHint section="Reflections (blog)">
          <p>
            "Technical problem-solving journeys and other random things" is doing the site's
            worldview real damage — it files
            <Link href="/blog/we-are-to-god-as-llms-are-to-us">We are to God as LLMs are to Us</Link>,
            the Sohn Jin-kwan biography, and the Christian ethics drafts under "random
            things." A third of your published writing is explicitly about faith, and the
            site currently apologizes for it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Subtlety means not announcing a worldview. It doesn't mean disowning the writing
            that carries it. Consider what these posts actually have in common with the
            Docker and AI-market ones — probably closer to "what I'm trying to understand"
            than to "random."
          </p>
        </DevHint>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-y-2 items-center justify-between">
          <h5 class="font-semibold text-black dark:text-white">
            Reflections
          </h5>
          <Link href="/blog">
            See all posts
          </Link>
        </div>
        <p class="animate">
          I frequently <a href="/blog">blog</a> about my technical problem-solving journeys and other random things.
        </p>
        <ul class="flex flex-col gap-4">
          {blog.map(post => (
            <li>
              <ArrowCard entry={post} />
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Connection: an invitation, not a transaction. -->
      <section class="animate space-y-6">
        <DevHint section="Connection (contact)">
          <p>
            This section already satisfies the model — "near and dear to your heart" and the
            offer to meet in person are an invitation to relationship, not a contact form.
            Nothing here needs rescuing.
          </p>
          <p>
            The one open question is the address: <code>tensorturtle@gmail.com</code> is the
            handle-based identity, while you now write from <code>jason@ayni.health</code>.
            Which one you offer a stranger is a small signal about whether this page is a
            persona or a person.
          </p>
        </DevHint>
        <article class="space-y-4">
          <p class="animate">
            The world is full of interesting problems to be solved.
            If you've got one that is near and dear to your heart, I want to hear from you.

            If you happen to be in Seoul, South Korea, we can meet up in person.
          </p>
        </article>
        <div class="flex items-baseline space-x-2">
          <Icon name="email" class="relative top-[3px]" />
          <a href="mailto:tensorturtle@gmail.com" class="hover:underline">tensorturtle@gmail.com</a>
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </Container>
</PageLayout>
